>Plus if you can't teleport they can't teleport right?

False.  The quest nemesis monsters, Rodney, master liches and arch liches,
Vlad the Impaler, and the demon lords and princes (and possibly a few
other monsters I've missed), all use the "tactics and strategy"
capabilities of Nethack, which permit them to perform controlled
teleports between vicinity-of-you and the up stairs at will.

If you zap your nemesis with a wand of teleport, you will wake him up
and break his meditation.  Furthermore, the "strategy" considerations
for your nemesis include the desire to possess your quest artifact.
Since, in your policy, the nemesis will be uninjured, the strategy code
will lead him to say "Aha!  This buffoon's Quest Artifact lies on the
floor *there*!" and teleport to wherever your quest artifact landed.

Best case: you teleport the nemesis, and get a double move.  This allows
you to teleport to where the quest artifact is; if your autopickup is
set up correctly you lift it off the floor forthwith.  Now, your nemesis
is *guaranteed* to get a move (it is impossible for the player to get a
triple move against any monster of current speed 12 or higher).  The
nemesis performs tactics-and-strategy evaluation, perceives that you
have the quest artifact, and teleports to you at zero time cost.

Having teleported next to you, the nemesis *immediately* attacks you.
All quest nemesis monsters have an AD_SAMU attack, which allows them to
steal your (but not anyone else's) quest artifact, the Bell of Opening,
the Candelabrum of Invocation, the Book of the Dead, or the Amulet of
Yendor.  If you have a good enough armour class, or the RNG likes you,
this attack will miss and you can teleport to the stairs; otherwise, the
quest artifact will get stolen and now you've got to fight your nemesis
for it.

